•     
Engage the mathematics
community. Represent Edfinity to
faculty, instructors, and department leadership at conferences (such as the
Joint Mathematics Meetings, MAA MathFest, and sectional meetings), workshops,
webinars, and campus conversations. You will be the person educators are glad to
talk to because you understand their world and can speak credibly about where
AI genuinely helps and where it does not.


•     
Help educators navigate
AI in the classroom. Many faculty are
actively figuring out what AI means for how they teach, grade, and assess. You
will be a trusted guide who runs sessions and conversations that help
instructors understand, evaluate, and make confident use of AI-powered tools in
their courses.


•     
Build relationships with
departments and instructors. Develop
genuine, lasting connections with the people who teach undergraduate
mathematics, understanding their goals, constraints, and questions about
teaching in an AI era.


•     
Shape product direction
by carrying the field's voice inward. Your
conversations with educators become a direct channel into what Edfinity builds
next. You will carry real classroom needs, and real concerns about AI, back to
our product team and influence the roadmap.


•     
Drive adoption and
academic success. Help departments
evaluate, adopt, and succeed with the platform, ensuring the institutions you
bring on genuinely thrive.


•     
Tell Edfinity's story
credibly. Translate what our AI-powered
platform actually does into terms that resonate with mathematicians, whether in
presentations, written pieces, or one-on-one conversations, with the precision
and honesty that a technical audience respects.



